    "summary": "High‑energy Playin’ sandwich and soaring Here Comes Sunshine",
    "blurb": "The show delivers a blistering Playin' > Uncle John's Band > Morning Dew palindrome with Jerry's crystal‑clear guitar and Phil’s booming bass. Highlights include a scorching Here Comes Sunshine and a seamless China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider transition. All available soundboard sources are pristine, making this a must‑hear performance.",
    "review": "The November 17, 1973 Pauley Pavilion concert captures the Dead at a creative peak. From the opening “Here Comes Sunshine” through a tight first set, the band builds momentum that explodes in the second‑set Playin' sandwich – an immaculate palindrome of Playing In The Band → Uncle John's Band → Morning Dew → Uncle John's Band → Playing In The Band that has become legendary among ’73 recordings. Jerry Garcia’s tone is described as “perfect” and his solos on China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider and throughout the sandwich are both soaring and deeply melodic, while Phil Lesh lays down a solid, sometimes bomb‑laden bass foundation that drives the improvisations forward. Keith Godchaux’s organ adds a jazzy undercurrent, especially on the China > Rider pairing, and Bill Kreutzmann locks in with tight hi‑hat work that propels the grooves. Bob Weir provides steady rhythm guitar and soulful vocals on tracks like Looks Like Rain and Brown Eyed Women. The soundboard mixes are uniformly excellent – crisp, balanced, and full of detail – allowing every nuance of this high‑energy show to shine.",
